\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Gyro rotation over the true image interval. (a) The released inertial windows hold 11 samples per image pair, read as ten 10\,ms steps, but the image interval is $\approx $104\,ms, so every edge loses $\approx $4\% of its rotation. (b) Pipeline. The lag and the IMU-to-camera rotation $\mathbf  {R}_{ci}$ are fitted on training drives only: the lag on a grid from $-80$ to $+80$\,ms (10\,ms, then 2.5\,ms steps) minimizing the median residual after a Kabsch alignment.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:timing}{{1}{3}{Gyro rotation over the true image interval. (a) The released inertial windows hold 11 samples per image pair, read as ten 10\,ms steps, but the image interval is $\approx $104\,ms, so every edge loses $\approx $4\% of its rotation. (b) Pipeline. The lag and the IMU-to-camera rotation $\mathbf {R}_{ci}$ are fitted on training drives only: the lag on a grid from $-80$ to $+80$\,ms (10\,ms, then 2.5\,ms steps) minimizing the median residual after a Kabsch alignment}{figure.1}{}}
